er_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input has one or more vertical columns of coloured cells, broken up by gaps. In the output those columns are erased. In their place, a centred \"pyramid\" sits on the bottom edge, directly under the column.\n\n**Evidence from the demonstrations:**\n- **Pair 1:** column x=6 holds 9 cells of colour 7, in pieces of 2, 3, 1 and 3 cells. The output is a pyramid at x=6 with rows of width 1, 3 and 5 from top to bottom. That is 9 cells, so the cell count is kept and the gaps do not matter.\n- **Pair 2:** 4 single cells become rows of width 1 and 3. Again 4 cells.\n- **Pair 3** shows how colours are ordered. From top to bottom the column reads: one 4, then three 7s, then five 6s. The pyramid's top row (width 1) is colour 4, the middle row (width 3) is 7, and the bottom row (width 5) is 6. So the column's cells, read top to bottom, fill the pyramid rows top to bottom. Row k has width 2k+1, centred on the column's x. The last row lands on the bottom edge of the grid.\n\n**Rule.** For each column that contains non-background cells:\n1. Collect its cells' colours from top to bottom.\n2. Split that list into chunks of 1, 3, 5, … cells.\n3. Erase the column.\n4. Stack the chunks as centred rows, the widest one on the bottom row of the grid.\n\nThis matches all three train pairs: 9 → 1+3+5, 4 → 1+3, and 1+5+3 cells in the colour order shown.\n\n**Applying it to the test (30×10):**\n- **Column x=2:** colours top to bottom are 2,2,2,2,1,1,1,1,1 (9 cells). This gives `2` on row 27, `2 2 2` on row 28 (x 1–3) and `1 1 1 1 1` on row 29 (x 0–4). It fits inside the grid.\n- **Column x=8:** four 7s. This gives `7` on row 28 and `7 7 7` on row 29 (x 7–9). It touches the right edge exactly.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The emitted canvases are:\n1. All columns cleared.\n2. One canvas per finished pyramid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n    cols = []\n    for x in range(W):\n        seq = [int(g[y, x]) for y in range(H) if g[y, x] != bg]\n        if seq:\n            cols.append((x, seq))\n            out[:, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase every coloured column; remember each column's colours read top to bottom.\")\n    for x, seq in cols:\n        # split into rows of width 1,3,5,...\n        rows = []\n        i, w = 0, 1\n        while i < len(seq):\n            rows.append(seq[i:i + w])\n            i += w\n            w += 2\n        n = len(rows)\n        for k, row in enumerate(rows):\n            y = H - n + k\n            half = k  # full row width is 2k+1\n            for j, c in enumerate(row):\n                xx = x - half + j\n                if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= xx < W:\n                    out[y, xx] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Stack column x={x}'s {len(seq)} cells as a centred pyramid (rows of width 1,3,5,...) on the bottom edge.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That is 9 cells, so the cell count is kept and the gaps do not matter."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f875c182c027cfb1197164e8fceb38f42cd770f4ccc2f4412df502ac52328e3c","task_sha256":"c576a70ec993c93fe11a3b787e814ada7e328354e378d0101043d5aebff771c4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
